Article 19 Intermediate People's Courts shall have jurisdiction over the following first instance civil cases:
(1) major cases involving foreign elements;
(2) cases that have a significant impact within their respective jurisdictions;
(3) cases that the Supreme People's Court designates as falling under the jurisdiction of Intermediate People's Courts.

Article 21 The Supreme People's Court shall have jurisdiction over the following first instance civil cases:
(1) cases that have a significant impact nationwide;
(2) cases it deems should be adjudicated by itself.

Article 23 The following civil actions shall be under the jurisdiction of the People's Court at the place of the plaintiff's domicile; if the plaintiff's domicile is inconsistent with the place of habitual residence, the action shall be under the jurisdiction of the People's Court at the plaintiff's place of habitual residence:
(1) actions concerning identity relations brought against a person who is not residing within the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(2) actions concerning identity relations brought against a person whose whereabouts are unknown or who has been declared missing;
(3) actions brought against a person who is subject to compulsory educational measures;
(4) actions brought against a person who is imprisoned.

Article 34 The following cases shall be subject to the exclusive jurisdiction of the People's Courts specified in this Article:
(1) an action arising from a real property dispute shall be under the jurisdiction of the People's Court at the place where the real property is located;
(2) an action arising from a dispute occurring during port operations shall be under the jurisdiction of the People's Court at the place where the port is located;
(3) an action arising from a dispute over succession to an estate shall be under the jurisdiction of the People's Court at the place of the decedent's domicile at the time of death or at the place where the principal estate is located.

Article 42 A People's Court shall not try the following civil cases by a single judge alone:
(1) Cases involving national interests or public interests;
(2) Cases involving group disputes which may affect social stability;
(3) Cases that are of wide public concern or have other relatively significant social impact;
(4) Cases that are of a new type or are difficult and complex;
(5) Cases that shall be tried by a collegial panel as provided by law; and
(6) Other cases that are not suitable for trial by a single judge alone.

Article 66 Evidence shall include:
(1) Statements of the parties;
(2) Documentary evidence;
(3) Physical evidence;
(4) Audio-visual materials;
(5) Electronic data;
(6) Testimony of witnesses;
(7) Expert opinions; and
(8) Records of inspection.
Evidence must be verified to be truthful before it may be used as a basis for finding facts.

Article 76 Upon notification by the People's Court, a witness shall appear in court to testify. Under any of the following circumstances, with the permission of the People's Court, the witness may testify by means of a written deposition, audio-visual transmission technology, or audio-visual materials:
(1) Unable to appear in court due to health reasons;
(2) Unable to appear in court due to long distance and inconvenient transportation;
(3) Unable to appear in court due to force majeure, such as natural disasters; or
(4) Unable to appear in court for other legitimate reasons.

Article 101 For the following cases where a mediation agreement is reached, the People's Court may not prepare a mediation document:
(1) divorce cases where reconciliation is achieved through mediation;
(2) cases where the adoptive relationship is maintained through mediation;
(3) cases that can be performed immediately;
(4) other cases for which a mediation document is not necessary.
An agreement for which no mediation document is necessary shall be recorded in the record, and after the record is signed or sealed by both parties, the judge, and the court clerk, it shall have legal effect.

Article 109 For the following cases, the People's Court may, upon the application of a party, rule to grant advance execution:
(1) cases seeking payment of alimony, support, child support, pension, or medical expenses;
(2) cases seeking payment of labor remuneration;
(3) cases where advance execution is necessary due to urgent circumstances.

Article 114 Where participants in proceedings or other persons commit any of the following acts, the People's Court may, depending on the seriousness of the circumstances, impose a fine or detention; if the act constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated in accordance with the law:
(1) forging or destroying important evidence, thereby obstructing the People's Court from trying a case;
(2) by means of violence, threats, or bribery, preventing a witness from testifying, or instigating, bribing, or coercing another person to give false testimony;
(3) concealing, transferring, selling, or destroying property that has been sealed up or distrained, or property that has been inventoried and ordered to be kept in custody, or transferring property that has been frozen;
(4) insulting, slandering, framing, assaulting, or taking revenge against judicial personnel, participants in proceedings, witnesses, interpreters, expert witnesses, inspectors, or persons assisting in enforcement;
(5) by means of violence, threats, or other methods, obstructing judicial personnel from performing their duties;
(6) refusing to perform a judgment or ruling of the People's Court that has already taken legal effect.
For an entity that commits any of the acts specified in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court may impose a fine or detention on its principal or the person directly responsible; if the act constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated in accordance with the law.

Article 117 Where an entity having the obligation to assist in investigation or enforcement commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may, in addition to ordering it to perform its obligation to assist, impose a fine:
(1) the relevant entity refuses or obstructs the People's Court from investigating and collecting evidence;
(2) the relevant entity, after receiving a notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, refuses to assist in inquiring, distraining, freezing, transferring, or converting property into money;
(3) the relevant entity, after receiving a notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, refuses to assist in withholding the income of the person subject to enforcement, handling the transfer of property rights certificates, or delivering relevant instruments, certificates, or other property;
(4) other instances of refusing to assist in enforcement.
For an entity that commits any of the acts specified in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court may impose a fine on its principal or the person directly responsible; if the entity still fails to perform its obligation to assist, detention may be imposed; and the court may submit a judicial suggestion to the supervisory authority or the relevant authority for disciplinary sanctions.

Article 122: The prosecution must meet the following conditions:
(1) The plaintiff is a citizen, legal person, or any other organization that has a direct interest in the case;
(2) There is a definite defendant;
(3) There are specific claims, facts, and reasons;
(4) The case falls within the scope of civil actions accepted by the People's Court and the jurisdiction of the People's Court hearing the case.

Article 124: The statement of complaint shall specify the following matters:
(1) The plaintiff's name, gender, age, ethnicity, occupation, work unit, domicile, and contact information; the name, domicile, and the name, position, and contact information of the legal representative or principal person in charge of a legal person or any other organization;
(2) The defendant's name, gender, work unit, domicile, and other information; the name, domicile, and other information of a legal person or any other organization;
(3) The claims and the facts and reasons on which they are based;
(4) The evidence and the sources of evidence, and the names and domiciles of witnesses.

Article 127: The People's Court shall handle the following prosecutions according to the circumstances:
(1) If, in accordance with the provisions of the Administrative Procedure Law, the case falls within the scope of administrative actions, the court shall inform the plaintiff to initiate an administrative action;
(2) If, in accordance with the provisions of the law, the parties have reached a written arbitration agreement to apply for arbitration and may not file a lawsuit with the People's Court, the court shall inform the plaintiff to apply for arbitration with the arbitration institution;
(3) If, in accordance with the provisions of the law, the dispute should be handled by another authority, the court shall inform the plaintiff to apply for resolution with the relevant authority;
(4) For a case not under the jurisdiction of this court, the court shall inform the plaintiff to file a lawsuit with the People's Court that has jurisdiction;
(5) For a case where a judgment, ruling, or mediation instrument has already become legally effective, and the parties file another lawsuit, the court shall inform the plaintiff to apply for a retrial, except for a ruling by the People's Court allowing the withdrawal of the lawsuit;
(6) For a case where, in accordance with the provisions of the law, a lawsuit may not be filed within a certain time limit, if a lawsuit is filed within the period during which prosecution is prohibited, the court shall not accept the case;
(7) For a divorce case where divorce is not allowed by judgment or reconciliation is reached through mediation, or a case where an adoption relationship is maintained by judgment or mediation, if no new circumstances or new reasons arise and the plaintiff files another lawsuit within six months, the court shall not accept the case.

Article 136: The People's Court shall handle the accepted cases according to the circumstances:
(1) If the parties have no dispute and the conditions for the expedited procedure are met, the case may be transferred to the expedited procedure;
(2) If mediation is possible before trial, mediation shall be used to timely resolve the dispute;
(3) Based on the circumstances of the case, determine whether to apply the summary procedure or the ordinary procedure;
(4) If a trial hearing is necessary, the court shall clarify the focus of the dispute by requiring the parties to exchange evidence and by other means.

Article 141: The court investigation shall be conducted in the following order:
(1) Statements by the parties;
(2) Informing witnesses of their rights and obligations, witnesses testifying, and reading the testimony of witnesses who are not present in court;
(3) Presenting documentary evidence, physical evidence, audio-visual materials, and electronic data;
(4) Reading the expert evaluation opinions;
(5) Reading the inspection records.

Article 144: The court debate shall be conducted in the following order:
(1) The plaintiff and its litigation agent make statements;
(2) The defendant and its litigation agent respond to the allegations;
(3) The third party and its litigation agent make statements or respond to the allegations;
(4) Mutual debate.
After the court debate is concluded, the presiding judge or the sole judge shall, in the order of plaintiff, defendant, and third party, solicit the final opinions of each party.

Article 149: The trial hearing may be postponed in any of the following circumstances:
(1) A party or other participant in the proceedings who must appear in court fails to appear for a justifiable reason;
(2) A party makes a last-minute request for withdrawal;
(3) It is necessary to summon a new witness to appear in court, to obtain new evidence, to conduct a new expert evaluation or inspection, or to supplement an investigation;
(4) Other circumstances that warrant a postponement.

Article 153: The action shall be suspended in any of the following circumstances:
(1) A party dies and it is necessary to wait for the heir to indicate whether to participate in the action;
(2) A party loses capacity for litigation and a legal representative has not yet been determined;
(3) A legal person or any other organization that is a party terminates and a bearer of rights and obligations has not yet been determined;
(4) A party is unable to participate in the action due to a force majeure event;
(5) The outcome of this case must be based on the outcome of another case, and that other case has not yet been concluded;
(6) Other circumstances that warrant the suspension of the action.
After the reason for suspension is eliminated, the action shall be resumed.

Article 154: The action shall be terminated in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The plaintiff dies without an heir, or the heir waives the right to sue;
(2) The defendant dies, leaving no estate and no person who shall bear the obligations;
(3) A party in a divorce case dies;
(4) A party in a case involving a claim for alimony, spousal support, child support, or dissolution of an adoption relationship dies.

Article 155: The written judgment shall specify the judgment result and the reasons for the judgment. The content of the written judgment shall include:
(1) The subject matter of the case, the claims, the disputed facts, and the reasons;
(2) The facts and reasons found by the judgment, the applicable laws, and the reasons;
(3) The judgment result and the allocation of litigation costs;
(4) The period for appeal and the court to which the appeal shall be filed.
The written judgment shall be signed by the adjudication personnel and the court clerk, and sealed with the seal of the People's Court.

Article 157: A ruling shall apply to the following matters:
(1) Refusal to accept a case;
(2) Objection to jurisdiction;
(3) Dismissal of a lawsuit;
(4) Preservation and advance execution;
(5) Permission or refusal of withdrawal of a lawsuit;
(6) Suspension or termination of an action;
(7) Correction of clerical errors in written judgments;
(8) Suspension or termination of enforcement;
(9) Revocation or refusal of enforcement of an arbitral award;
(10) Refusal of enforcement of a debt instrument that has been granted compulsory enforcement effect by a notarization authority;
(11) Other matters requiring resolution by ruling.
A ruling under items (1) to (3) of the preceding paragraph may be appealed.
The written ruling shall specify the result of the ruling and the reasons for the ruling. The written ruling shall be signed by the adjudication personnel and the court clerk, and sealed with the seal of the People's Court. If a ruling is made orally, it shall be recorded in the record.

Article 166 A People's Court shall not apply the small-claim procedure to the following civil cases:
(1) Cases concerning personal relationships or confirmation of property rights;
(2) Cases involving foreign elements;
(3) Cases requiring evaluation, expert evaluation, or where there is objection to pre-litigation evaluation or expert evaluation results;
(4) Cases where one party's whereabouts are unknown;
(5) Cases where a counterclaim is filed;
(6) Other cases unsuitable for trial under the small-claim procedure.

Article 177 After hearing an appealed case, the second-instance People's Court shall handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts found and the application of law in the original judgment or ruling are correct, the appeal shall be dismissed by judgment or ruling, and the original judgment or ruling shall be affirmed;
(2) If the facts found or the application of law in the original judgment or ruling are erroneous, the judgment or ruling shall be amended, set aside, or varied according to law;
(3) If the basic facts found in the original judgment are unclear, the original judgment shall be set aside by ruling and remanded to the original People's Court for retrial, or the judgment shall be amended after the facts are clarified;
(4) If the original judgment omits a party or is rendered by default in violation of the law, or there are other serious violations of legal procedures, the original judgment shall be set aside by ruling and remanded to the original People's Court for retrial.
If a party files an appeal against a judgment rendered by the original People's Court upon remand, the second-instance People's Court may not remand the case again.

Article 205 Where a mediation agreement is reached through mediation by a mediation organization established according to law and an application is made for judicial confirmation, both parties shall, within thirty days from the date the mediation agreement takes effect, jointly file the application with the following People's Courts:
(1) Where a People's Court has invited a mediation organization to conduct pre-litigation mediation, the application shall be filed with the court that made the invitation;
(2) Where a mediation organization conducts mediation on its own, the application shall be filed with the Primary People's Court of the place of the domicile of the parties, the place where the subject matter is located, or the place where the mediation organization is located; if the dispute involved in the mediation agreement shall be under the jurisdiction of an Intermediate People's Court, the application shall be filed with the corresponding Intermediate People's Court.

Article 211 A People's Court shall conduct a retrial if the application of a party falls under any of the following circumstances:
(1) There is new evidence sufficient to overturn the original judgment or ruling;
(2) The basic facts determined in the original judgment or ruling lack evidentiary support;
(3) The main evidence for the facts determined in the original judgment or ruling is forged;
(4) The main evidence for the facts determined in the original judgment or ruling was not cross-examined;
(5) The party, due to objective reasons, could not collect on its own the main evidence necessary for the trial of the case and filed a written application with the People's Court for investigation and collection, but the court failed to investigate and collect it;
(6) The application of law in the original judgment or ruling is indeed erroneous;
(7) The composition of the trial organization is illegal, or a judicial officer who shall withdraw according to law fails to do so;
(8) A person without capacity for litigation was not represented by a legal agent in the proceedings, or a party that should have participated in the proceedings did not do so due to reasons not attributable to the party or the party's litigation agent;
(9) In violation of legal provisions, the party was deprived of the right to debate;
(10) A default judgment was rendered without service of a summons;
(11) The original judgment or ruling omits or exceeds the claims;
(12) The legal document based on which the original judgment or ruling was rendered has been revoked or modified;
(13) The judicial officers involved in the trial of the case committed acts of graft or bribery, engaged in favoritism or other malpractice, or rendered a judgment perverting the law.

Article 220 Under any of the following circumstances, a party may apply to the People's Procuratorate for a procuratorial recommendation or a protest:
(1) The People's Court dismisses the application for retrial;
(2) The People's Court fails to rule on the application for retrial within the prescribed time limit;
(3) The judgment or ruling rendered upon retrial contains a manifest error.
The People's Procuratorate shall examine the application of the party within three months and decide whether to issue or not to issue a procuratorial recommendation or file a protest. The party shall not apply again to the People's Procuratorate for a procuratorial recommendation or protest.

Article 248 For an award made by an arbitration institution established according to law, if one party fails to perform, the other party may apply to the competent People's Court for enforcement. The People's Court to which application is made shall enforce the award.
If the respondent provides evidence proving that the arbitration award falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall, after forming a collegial panel to examine and verify, rule not to enforce the award:
(1) The parties did not include an arbitration clause in the contract or did not subsequently reach a written arbitration agreement;
(2) The matters decided are beyond the scope of the arbitration agreement or the arbitration institution has no authority to arbitrate;
(3) The formation of the arbitration tribunal or the arbitration procedure violated statutory procedures;
(4) The evidence on which the award is based is forged;
(5) The other party concealed evidence sufficient to affect the fair award from the arbitration institution;
(6) An arbitrator engaged in graft, bribery, favoritism, or perversion of law in making the award.
If the People's Court determines that enforcement of the award violates the public interest, it shall rule not to enforce it.
The ruling shall be served on the parties and the arbitration institution.
If the arbitration award is ruled not to be enforced by the People's Court, the parties may reapply for arbitration based on the written arbitration agreement reached by them, or may institute proceedings in the People's Court.

Article 267 In any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall rule to suspend enforcement:
(1) The applicant indicates that enforcement may be postponed;
(2) A person not involved in the case raises a well-founded objection to the subject matter of enforcement;
(3) A citizen who is a party dies and it is necessary to await the successor to succeed to the rights or assume the obligations;
(4) A legal person or other organization that is a party terminates and the successor of rights and obligations has not yet been determined;
(5) Other circumstances where the People's Court considers that enforcement shall be suspended.
Enforcement shall resume after the circumstances for suspension disappear.

Article 268 In any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall rule to terminate enforcement:
(1) The applicant withdraws the application;
(2) The legal document on the basis of which enforcement is carried out is revoked;
(3) The citizen who is the person subject to execution dies, leaving no estate for enforcement and no person to assume the obligation;
(4) The right holder in a case for recovery of alimony, support, or child support dies;
(5) The citizen who is the person subject to execution is unable to repay the loan due to financial difficulties, has no source of income, and has lost the ability to work;
(6) Other circumstances where the People's Court considers that enforcement shall be terminated.

Article 279 The following civil cases shall be under the exclusive jurisdiction of the People's Court:
(1) Actions instituted arising from disputes over the establishment, dissolution, or liquidation of a legal person or other organization established within the territory of the People's Republic of China, or over the validity of resolutions made by such legal person or other organization;
(2) Actions instituted arising from disputes relating to the validity of intellectual property rights examined and granted within the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(3) Actions instituted arising from disputes over the performance of contracts for Chinese-foreign equity joint ventures, contracts for Chinese-foreign contractual joint ventures, or contracts for Chinese-foreign cooperative exploration and development of natural resources within the territory of the People's Republic of China.

If, in a foreign-related civil action accepted by the People's Court, the defendant raises an objection to jurisdiction and the following circumstances concurrently exist, the People's Court may rule to dismiss the prosecution and inform the plaintiff to institute an action before a more convenient foreign court:
(1) the basic facts in dispute of the case do not occur within the territory of the People's Republic of China, and it is manifestly inconvenient for the People's Court to adjudicate the case and for the parties to participate in the action;
(2) there is no agreement between the parties choosing the jurisdiction of the People's Court;
(3) the case does not fall within the exclusive jurisdiction of the People's Court;
(4) the case does not involve the sovereignty, security, or public interests of the People's Republic of China;
(5) it is more convenient for a foreign court to adjudicate the case.
After the prosecution is dismissed by ruling, if the foreign court refuses to exercise jurisdiction over the dispute, or fails to take necessary measures to adjudicate the case, or fails to conclude the case within a reasonable period, and the party institutes an action before the People's Court again, the People's Court shall accept the case.

The People's Court may use the following methods for service of litigation documents on a party who has no domicile within the territory of the People's Republic of China:
(1) service in the manner prescribed in the international treaties concluded or jointly acceded to by the country where the person on whom service is to be made is located and the People's Republic of China;
(2) service through diplomatic channels;
(3) for a person on whom service is to be made who is a national of the People's Republic of China, entrusting the embassy or consulate of the People's Republic of China in the country where the person is located to serve on the person's behalf;
(4) service on the litigation agent authorized by the person on whom service is to be made in the case;
(5) service on the wholly-owned enterprise, representative office, branch office, or business agent authorized to accept service established by the person on whom service is to be made within the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(6) if the person on whom service is to be made is a foreigner or a stateless person, who serves as the legal representative or principal responsible person of a legal person or other organization established within the territory of the People's Republic of China, and is a co-defendant with such legal person or other organization, service on such legal person or other organization;
(7) if the person on whom service is to be made is a foreign legal person or other organization, whose legal representative or principal responsible person is within the territory of the People's Republic of China, service on such legal representative or principal responsible person;
(8) if the law of the country where the person on whom service is to be made is located permits service by mail, service may be made by mail; if the receipt of service is not returned after three full months from the date of mailing, but it can be sufficiently determined based on all circumstances that service has been made, the date upon which the period expires shall be deemed the date of service;
(9) service by electronic means that can confirm receipt by the person on whom service is to be made, unless prohibited by the law of the country where the person is located;
(10) service by other means agreed by the person on whom service is to be made, unless prohibited by the law of the country where the person is located.
If none of the above methods of service can be used, service by public announcement shall be made; sixty days after the date of the issuance of the announcement, service shall be deemed to have been made.

If the evidence that a party applies to the People's Court to investigate and collect is located outside the territory of the People's Republic of China, the People's Court may investigate and collect it in the manner prescribed in the international treaties concluded or jointly acceded to by the country where the evidence is located and the People's Republic of China, or through diplomatic channels.
If not prohibited by the law of the country where the evidence is located, the People's Court may investigate and collect evidence using the following methods:
(1) for a party or witness who is a national of the People's Republic of China, entrusting the embassy or consulate of the People's Republic of China in the country where such party or witness is located to take evidence on its behalf;
(2) with the consent of both parties, taking evidence through instant communication tools;
(3) taking evidence by other means agreed by both parties.

For an award made by a foreign-related arbitration institution of the People's Republic of China, if the respondent presents evidence proving that the arbitration award falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall, after examination and verification by a collegial panel formed by the People's Court, rule that the award shall not be enforced:
(1) the parties have not included an arbitration clause in the contract or have not subsequently concluded a written arbitration agreement;
(2) the respondent has not received notice of the appointment of an arbitrator or of the arbitration proceedings, or was unable to present its case due to reasons for which the respondent is not responsible;
(3) the composition of the arbitration tribunal or the arbitration procedure was not in accordance with the arbitration rules;
(4) the matters decided in the award fall outside the scope of the arbitration agreement or the arbitration institution has no authority to arbitrate.
If the People's Court determines that the enforcement of the award would be contrary to the social and public interests, the People's Court shall rule that the award shall not be enforced.

With respect to a legally effective judgment or ruling made by a foreign court for which recognition and enforcement is applied for or requested, the People's Court shall, after examination, rule not to recognize and enforce it if any of the following circumstances exists:
(1) in accordance with the provisions of Article 301 of this Law, the foreign court has no jurisdiction over the case;
(2) the respondent has not been legally summoned, or although legally summoned, has not been given a reasonable opportunity to present its case or to be heard, or a party who lacks legal capacity has not been properly represented;
(3) the judgment or ruling was obtained by fraud;
(4) the People's Court has already made a judgment or ruling on the same dispute, or has already recognized a judgment or ruling made by a court of a third state on the same dispute;
(5) the judgment or ruling violates the fundamental principles of the laws of the People's Republic of China or harms national sovereignty, security, or social and public interests.

The People's Court shall determine that a foreign court has no jurisdiction over a case if any of the following circumstances exists:
(1) the foreign court has no jurisdiction over the case under its own law, or although it has jurisdiction under its own law, it has no appropriate connection with the dispute involved in the case;
(2) the foreign court's jurisdiction violates the provisions of this Law on exclusive jurisdiction;
(3) the foreign court's jurisdiction violates an agreement between the parties exclusively selecting the jurisdiction of a court.
